For this reason, we also develop a model based on word extraction which seeks to find a subset of words in $D$ and their optimal ordering so as to form a summary $\mathbf {y}_s = (w^{\prime }_1, \cdots , w^{\prime }_k), w^{\prime }_i \in D$ . Compared to sentence extraction which is a sequence labeling problem, this task occupies the middle ground between full abstractive summarization which can exhibit a wide range of rewrite operations and extractive summarization which exhibits none. We formulate word extraction as a language generation task with an output vocabulary restricted to the original document. In our supervised setting, the training goal is to maximize the likelihood of the generated sentences, which can be further decomposed by enforcing conditional dependencies among their constituent words: 

